WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:05.040
After an infamously troubled production and
months of promotion and speculation, Solo:

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.000
A Star Wars Story has finally arrived.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:12.019
It features great action, top-notch special
effects, and a handful of genuinely memorable

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:12.700
performances.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.040
Still, it's far from perfect.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.780
Buckle up for hyperspace - there are going
to be plenty of spoilers ahead as we take

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.620
a deep dive into the five best and five worst
things about Solo: A Star Wars Story.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:25.100
Best: Lando steals the show

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.810
Who didn't see this one coming?

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:30.910
A lot of people have been excited to see Donald
Glover's debut as legendary wheeler-dealer

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:32.000
Lando Calrissian.

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:34.360
Fortunately, Glover's performance delivers.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:36.159
He's everything a young Lando should be.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.340
He's suave and talented, yes, but also constantly
posturing and clearly insecure.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:44.859
Glover is at his best in Solo when acting
out Lando's more narcissistic tendencies…

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:46.320
and reveling in his own fashion.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.160
I have an everyday cape, a sister's wedding cape, intergalactic president's day cape."

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:56.180
He even nails the tone and inflection of Lando's
speech without ever doing an outright impersonation.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:00.880
Throw in his chemistry with his droid copilot
L3-37 and you've got a character who steals

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.000
every scene he's in.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.140
Worst: Lighten up

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:08.240
Solo has a couple of moments that are very
well framed, but as a whole the movie is a

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:09.580
bit of a visual letdown.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:14.020
This is particularly evident in the first
act, which takes place mostly in seedy alleys

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:14.820
and crime dens.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.820
These sequences are terribly lit to the point
that it's nearly impossible to follow the

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:19.320
action.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.760
There's a difference between dim lighting
and bad lighting.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:25.939
It's not an issue present throughout the entirety
of the film, but it doesn't get much better,

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:26.440
either.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:30.520
There's some competence to the cinematography,
but it never wows or feels as though it has

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:31.020
purpose.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:32.630
Best: Ante up

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:37.100
The Star Wars saga is filled with some of
the most memorable action of all time.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:40.979
Lightsaber duels and epic battle sequences
are the franchise's bread and butter.

00:01:40.979 --> 00:01:44.819
So it's interesting that the most memorable
scene in Solo doesn't feature so much as a

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:45.320
blaster.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:49.719
It's simply two over-inflated egos sitting
across from each other at a table and egging

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:50.460
one another on.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:54.820
The moment Han and Lando meet for the first
time immediately feels as momentous as it

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:55.320
ought to.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:59.340
There's a palpable tension as the two size
one another up, and once the card game begins,

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.720
it cranks it into lightspeed.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:05.180
When Lando finally lays down a full sabacc
hand, it feels like the final crescendo of

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:06.020
a symphony.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:10.560
Solo's train heist and the thrilling double-crosses
in the finale can't compare to Lando and Han

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.300
sitting down for a game of cards.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.820
Worst: Dryden Vos disappoints

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:18.820
Even when the franchise is at its worst, Star
Wars has a knack for great villains.

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:23.709
We're not saying Solo is the worst, but its
primary antagonist, crime boss Dryden Vos,

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:26.349
definitely isn't going into the Hall of Fame
any time soon.

00:02:26.349 --> 00:02:30.290
You can't help but feel a little bored with
the character, even without the knowledge

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:34.099
that the character was originally going to
be a half-animal creature performed by Michael

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:34.920
K. Williams.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.360
"He was half mountain lion, half human."

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:42.580
Paul Bettany is an immensely talented actor
and seems to be doing the best he can with

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:47.320
the material, but a suave, violent crime boss
is nothing we haven't seen in a hundred movies

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:47.820
before.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.200
Best: Alden Ehrenreich sticks the landing

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.930
It's not a stretch to say that Han is one
of the most beloved movie characters of all

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:57.260
time - thanks in no small part to Harrison
Ford's performance.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.460
Ford is inseparable from Han, and any actor
stepping into the role has some big shoes

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:02.020
to fill.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:06.860
Despite those massive expectations, Ehrenreich
does the seemingly impossible in Solo: he

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:08.760
makes you forget he's not Harrison Ford.

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:12.720
Ehrenreich never seems to be doing a Ford
impression, which would have been an understandable

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:13.580
approach to the role.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:14.960
Instead he makes it his own.

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:19.790
His Han is still witty and caustic, but in
a way that feels like youthful bravado rather

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:20.300
than scorn.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:23.460
He also gives Han a naive optimism that plays
out beautifully.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.000
Worst: The first act struggles

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:30.140
Solo's troubled production is common knowledge
at this point, and the first act of the film

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:34.069
is the portion that seems to have been most
affected by the film's production issues and

00:03:34.069 --> 00:03:38.790
reshoots.Various tones and moments are struggling
for dominance, which is to be expected from

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:40.500
a movie with multiple directors.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:44.340
Nothing about the first 20 minutes of the
film really works, and it's largely because

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.440
it doesn't seem to have any sense of where
it stands.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.300
Once Han, Chewie, and Beckett board Vos' ship,
Solo finds itself, but it takes longer than

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:52.200
it should to get there.

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:54.180
Best: Worldbuilding

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.840
Solo is at its most engaging when it's building
the criminal underworld of Star Wars.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:02.900
Crimson Dawn, a syndicate of mob bosses who
run the shadier corners of the galaxy, is

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:06.240
an immediately intriguing concept ripe for
further exploration.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:09.380
And the fact that the outfit is headed by
Darth Maul is even better.

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:14.360
On top of that, Solo's introduction of Enfys
Nest and her Cloud Riders, a gang of renegade

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:17.240
refugees, is a fantastic piece of new Star
Wars lore.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:21.800
These bits of expansion come in small pieces,
but as a whole form a very engaging look at

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.000
corners of this galaxy we've never seen before.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.140
Worst: Over-explained

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.060
The ways Solo builds on Star Wars are great.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:33.400
But it also goes out of its way to explain
details perhaps best left to the imagination.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:37.620
The most prominent example is the Kessel Run,
in which Han and the crew make the run, only

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:40.800
narrowly avoiding death by giant tentacle
creature, is awesome.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.040
But finding out what the Kessel Run is to
begin with still feels somewhat unnecessary.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:49.220
Similarly, certain aspects of Han's origin
are inevitable, like how he and Chewbacca

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:50.220
come together.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:53.460
But did we really need to know how he came
into possession of his signature blaster?

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:57.960
The abundance of explanation that nobody asked
for in Solo serves little purpose — and

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:01.320
ultimately shrinks some of the possibilities
in its massive universe.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:03.180
Best: Bringing it home

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:06.440
You expect Solo to have a solid plot twist
or two.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:08.360
This is a movie about criminals, after all.

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:12.630
And there's a major plot twist that moves
the final act into motion, one that's legitimately

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:17.420
surprising: Enfys Nest and her Cloud Riders
aren't smugglers at all, but refugees forming

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:19.900
the first seeds of what will become the Rebel
Alliance.

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:23.780
There's always a war to be fought in these
movies, and Solo seems like the first installment

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:25.860
in the franchise whose focus is elsewhere.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:30.200
Enfys Nest reminds the protagonists of Solo
that not only is there a war out there but,

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:32.340
consciously or not, they're playing a part
in it.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:35.400
Without this, Solo is just a fun space adventure.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:37.300
With it, it's a proper Star Wars movie.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:39.560
Worst: It doesn't feel necessary

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:43.240
When you get right down to it, Han Solo never
needed an origin story.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:46.550
He isn't a figure shrouded in mystery like
Boba Fett.

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:49.930
He's just a talented pilot who gets himself
wrapped up in a fight for the fate of the

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:50.430
galaxy.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:55.020
Everything a great origin story needs is already
present in Han's arc in the original trilogy

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:59.810
- his journey from cynical smuggler to true
believer in the noble cause of the Rebellion.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:01.570
So why does this movie exist?

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:05.660
That's ultimately Solo's biggest flaw: it
fails to justify its own existence.

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:09.500
None of this means you'll regret seeing it,
but at no point does any of this become anything

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:13.800
truly special, or answer the question of why
this Star Wars Story needed to be told.

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:15.060
Thanks for watching!

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:17.780
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:19.920
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

